9}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570138} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1dab20-875a-5ecd-83ee-e9779ffd70fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6dff94b8-5d8c-56b5-9363-d9107e29b4d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15811" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $y$ on the graph $y=|x+4|-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x+4|-1$ has $h=-4$ and $k=-1.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $4$ units *left* and $1$ unit *down*.\n\n![](images/45/45411bac1f347093b8b9931e1f8f8bee4f1eb411b6256a4c56d0ba33cffe603e.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $-1.$ This occurs at the point $(-4,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "a2af62b9-442c-506b-9cce-b6a9e9270c22" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5d6999-dacf-585b-9763-55851b2e5a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570541} :answer-field/id #uuid "aecd1abd-2502-5a3e-aece-0d12a409100b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b65f25b-10a0-5e3f-8752-11a285fdf596" :question/math-academy-id "q-222360" :question/problem "The minimum value of the function $y=|x+3|+2$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x+3|+2$ has $h=-3$ and $k=2.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $3$ units *left* and $2$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/81/81bace946b2fb425efa7fd7c54eb53ec353be5d9ef1516d9cf24e2c13656a60b.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "c6cb8059-d840-5375-a89b-c4dc968b16b1"}] :activity/title "Absolute Value Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570110}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180636} :activity/id #uuid "d591686e-75aa-506f-aade-856d6dea793a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049495 :topic/id #uuid "f9324acf-7bff-528d-bb24-b67ba475ce6a" :topic/math-academy-id 3746} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180660 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we can use ratios to find quantities that aren't explicitly given in the ratio.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the ratio of boys to girls in the fifth grade is $3: 4.$ If there are $15$ boys, how many students are there in total?\n\nNotice that the \"total number of students\" doesn't appear in the ratio. However, we can find the number of students by adding the total number of boys and the number of girls.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe know that there are $15$ boys, so we want to make a numerator of $15.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}4}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}15}}{{\\color{red}20}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $15$ boys in the fifth grade, there must also be $20$ girls. So the total number of students is \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{red}20} = 35\\,.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca3cabae-8ebe-5879-b608-9d0ebdb233fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2051 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4a7f682-c223-50f1-a3eb-6b1f79aad9b0" :step/math-academy-id 637169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179494 :question/id #uuid "02ae40ba-a02c-5025-9597-1968322f2421" :question/math-academy-id "e-2279"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e44aa33-0aca-5cb2-8c89-6391240beed8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050174}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567027}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Amount Given a Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "91f76525-9d6e-5ea9-a6a3-b6249f246583" :step/math-academy-id 637170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179495 :question/id #uuid "4aeb615e-16a1-5170-be5e-fe0f63a5d895" :question/math-academy-id "e-2280"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5406de3-483e-56c2-b9c6-73de6769552e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567084}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Amount Given a Ratio as a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "5f14a9d9-61a5-5432-ad78-2673e2e5e50e" :step/math-academy-id 637173}] :activity/title "Further Reasoning With Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179889}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516890} :activity/id #uuid "23866e55-455f-5349-a24f-744eddf74337" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049495 :topic/id #uuid "f9324acf-7bff-528d-bb24-b67ba475ce6a" :topic/math-academy-id 3746} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19618e12-debd-5b4e-9bf1-429e65d1a0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330167} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4d2098-760f-5acc-84b4-40651c95e415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330758} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdcbebb-943f-5a2c-92b4-c3828824f1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330771} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4abe4a-1493-5b17-9061-c2a59e27d5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331053} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf4ef6e-5e9b-5fbd-8000-7085f8f93e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331053} :answer-field/id #uuid "cecfd35c-e905-59a9-a911-bac01e2f48fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9aff327a-3ab5-5bdb-b2ab-437fac7b7394" :question/math-academy-id "q-59895" :question/problem "There are $15$ students in a class, and $\\frac{2}{5}$ of them wear glasses. How many students **do not** wear glasses?" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the ratio of students who wear glasses to the total number of students is $\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of students who wear glasses, and the denominator represents the total number of students.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $15$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 3}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{6}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $6$ students who wear glasses. \n\nSo the number of students who don't wear glasses is\n\n$$\n15-6=9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "351d27f8-ae58-54cf-867a-22cc22f2bf67" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29fbf3dc-a47e-54b1-bcf8-7e68c453ce58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516198} {:answer/id #uuid "33b33412-cf90-51cd-bf23-bd54dfd122f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516220} {:answer/id #uuid "635a14ed-c473-5b21-9e9c-024325325da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516327} {:answer/id #uuid "d4530301-1e8c-5fda-aec9-3766286b1b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516559} {:answer/id #uuid "eddc115b-1150-555f-bd2f-52f8639641ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516604} :answer-field/id #uuid "d304b13f-6fc1-5cd7-bca3-7aa0d027956e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7dd5c31e-e5f0-51dc-a3ec-12e73e8808ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-147064" :question/problem "The ratio of sheep to goats in Manuel's herd is $3:1.$\nIf Manuel has $30$ sheep, how many animals are in the herd in total?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $3:1$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of sheep, and the denominator represents the number of goats.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $30$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 10}{1\\times 10}=\\frac{30}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Manuel has $30$ sheep and $10$ goats. So, the total number of animals in the herd is \n\n$$\n30+10=40.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c440cbf8-556c-5d78-8335-881bde443fbb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f71bb97-ece8-546e-a7a7-8359437ebda2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516320} {:answer/id #uuid "701eba46-6034-5191-b8c0-f4010b286be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516363} {:answer/id #uuid "70b5c337-134d-5b23-8234-a694bfeab0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516365} {:answer/id #uuid "bff2951a-541e-5f2c-b035-f5b17ef671ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516519} {:answer/id #uuid "dc72bee6-483f-5536-ad4b-5f542ab698ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516365} :answer-field/id #uuid "c50e1709-fb35-5c84-a6d5-30b4b5bd93b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bea17d69-eb40-57fe-baa2-90eb85105abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-60197" :question/problem "In an electronics store, the ratio of the number of cell phones to the total number of gadgets sold (cell phones and computers) is $\\frac{8}{11}.$ If $16$ cell phones were sold in a week, how many computers were sold?" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the ratio of the number of cell phones sold to the number of gadgets (cell phones and computers) sold is $\\frac{8}{11}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cell phones sold and the denominator represents the number of cell phones and computers sold.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $16$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{8\\times 2}{11\\times 2}=\\frac{16}{22}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of cell phones and computers sold that week was $22.$ Of this amount, the number of computers sold was\n\n$$\n22-16=6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe452996-f813-52b6-bce9-784c946d6846" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516960}}] :activity/title "Further Reasoning With Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516089}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567151} :activity/id #uuid "31327c94-86dc-5676-811e-1fced1bc3805" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049495 :topic/id #uuid "f9324acf-7bff-528d-bb24-b67ba475ce6a" :topic/math-academy-id 3746} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae5b0a4-8499-5310-9fc9-89eb76077c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566483} {:answer/id #uuid "61d78eef-0ef1-51d8-8f56-e304ece40893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566549} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4f278b-c6db-535e-ae2f-8c165187f9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566606} {:answer/id #uuid "86f960f4-d56e-5a35-94bf-96e675c7f1ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566618} {:answer/id #uuid "e8840b2e-dee6-5081-a059-47e9916237a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566549} :answer-field/id #uuid "f573bee3-51b2-5b6f-b283-d4ab5524dc98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26aa6167-d445-5a62-a697-315c80098811" :question/math-academy-id "q-60142" :question/problem "An animal shelter houses only dogs and cats. The ratio of dogs to cats is $4:3.$ If there are $32$ dogs, what is the total number of animals in the shelter?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $4:3$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of dogs and the denominator represents the number of cats.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $32$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $8:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 8}{3\\times 8}=\\frac{32}{24}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $32$ dogs and $24$ cats. So the total number of animals in the animal shelter is\n\n$$\n32+24=56.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23c11523-25f4-517f-9af8-e0e0997771bb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2017ff24-0581-50a6-a0e4-897c730b1b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330223} {:answer/id #uuid "39e19669-c003-5b81-804b-0508b1684a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330469} {:answer/id #uuid "7301049a-cded-5985-8144-7a673af44c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330985} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8cf9cd-ccd6-560f-bcac-033dc77df751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331190} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b091f7-dcf4-5b11-8f44-fd1a1d60d02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331671} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe8cef3-8694-5862-a34e-cf2a94036fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "46da9d15-27a6-531e-bac7-0a77b34580ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-60139" :question/problem "One day, $20$ adults and some children attended a music festival at Beethoven Park. If the ratio of adults to children was $4:7$, how many people attended the park that day?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $4:7$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of adults and the denominator represents the number of children.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $20$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 5}{7\\times 5}=\\frac{20}{35}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $20$ adults and $35$ children attended that day. Thus, the total number of people who went to the park that day was\n\n$$\n20+35=55.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "43965199-ae00-5566-87af-7df0f51980f8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ec7672a-1d0f-5d71-b116-7db8117aafa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330944} {:answer/id #uuid "81a88acf-af5c-553e-a384-8980c215f9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331098} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa9c985-6a3b-5722-9594-ce42ea188b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331788} {:answer/id #uuid "d4679159-09b6-5c1f-b452-67fa2f2362ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331834} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b14486-1576-5997-825e-fcc3df8ad4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330944} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc59fb8d-0cba-5bea-9780-04e655ab0928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6604770-26c5-5386-84c1-792391047167" :question/math-academy-id "q-59887" :question/problem "The ratio of girls to boys in a chess club is $2:1.$ If there are $10$ girls, then how many members does the club have in total?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $2:1$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of girls, and the denominator represents the number of boys.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $10$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{10}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $10$ girls and $5$ boys in the club. So the total number of members is \n\n$$\n10+5=15.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1f65a0e-623f-5ed6-b0e3-dbdf6d12bc49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567125}}] :activity/title "Further Reasoning With Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566352}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097467} :activity/id #uuid "8f327b60-e0cf-5553-a69d-9f75b2352ae4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603 :topic/id #uuid "03d5463d-8db1-5e89-8946-23139052e041" :topic/math-academy-id 1100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097828 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors, we can compute the sum by placing the head of one vector at the tail of the other.\n\nFor example, let's consider the two vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ below.\n\n![](images/04/049f79ae22e5931a7ceba7f73fef5cb6bc38d17c2a45e7186d57f8760b02488a.png)\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we need to draw the second vector $\\textbf{b}$ starting from the head of the first vector $\\textbf{a}.$ Now, the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that we get when we join the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/98/98a42bc3ecf7071630acc7d29349f75f77b20a9cc1c2c34382161cc79b47b4df.png)\n\nWe call this method the **triangle law of addition**. The vector obtained by adding two or more vectors is sometimes called the **resultant**." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1d80471-b824-5cac-9146-15b6e8b910d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2901 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbd21225-959b-5fe0-a9cb-fd7a77bebb11" :step/math-academy-id 15685 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097470}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096642 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096439 :question/id #uuid "96cb0487-40a4-5942-aa43-6ca61830bde0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48176a87-796e-5637-9552-4f7fccdeee6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050194}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510474}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "d2226c33-40b3-550e-9ab3-8359f4445fdd" :step/math-academy-id 15688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096441 :question/id #uuid "6db52d38-e14d-5424-9b54-af258ad7551c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4274"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db159911-4e9a-563e-9a3d-58d4e73dc862" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510477}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two Opposite Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "c5e4bcd7-3f24-565d-8d90-7d783587b66c" :step/math-academy-id 15690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097490}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097830 :tutorial/content "Given a vector $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ the **opposite vector** is the vector $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ that has the same length but goes in the opposite direction.\n\n![](images/65/6591fd004ba2b74c2651182ce0c70302ac789d868389d057a502b8e98ac61fab.png)\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=-\\overrightarrow{BA}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum of two opposite vectors,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA},\n$$\n\nwe can use the triangle law of addition.\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ to the head of $\\overrightarrow{BA}.$ Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head are the same point, $A.$ We denote that vector as\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA} = \\overrightarrow{AA}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is called the **zero vector**. The zero vector has no direction and zero magnitude. With the zero vector, the head coincides with the tail." :tutorial/id #uuid "ff30d4cd-a46e-5658-aada-2885298012f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2903 :tutorial/title "Opposite Vectors and the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "9589b39e-7e79-59cb-a04d-4c80d504257a" :step/math-academy-id 15691 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096443 :question/id #uuid "77d2bd5d-5f79-5204-8c65-395ef6414225" :question/math-academy-id "e-4276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9ae0161-d016-5aef-8c11-020047c66bbd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510459}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding More Than Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "79ccec21-3796-56f3-a7d4-24bb46c252eb" :step/math-academy-id 15693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096453 :question/id #uuid "5fade14b-9fa1-5777-afe3-c061907218f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4359"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711d0e70-a248-5f93-a836-43bed5435d4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510437}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "9525b995-7556-5826-b108-58b033c286e1" :step/math-academy-id 16022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097838 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use the triangle law to add two vectors. We can also use the triangle law to subtract two vectors.\n\nThe trick is to interpret the subtraction as the addition of a negative vector. For example, the subtraction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the addition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b}),\n$$\n\nwhere $-\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9e3c330e-f67a-51e0-abd7-144acc20080b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2960 :tutorial/title "Using the Triangle Law for Subtraction"} :step/id #uuid "e1b5149f-330e-5b4d-8235-f3c9ff3b5ad7" :step/math-academy-id 16023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097489}}] :activity/title "The Triangle Law for the Addition of Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096880}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116356} :activity/id #uuid "c1204165-46fa-568c-a8e1-e13727d2be23" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049732 :topic/id #uuid "0f13295d-4ece-5423-b035-ea84905ef389" :topic/math-academy-id 1629} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116434 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to interpret various aspects of an exponential decay expression or function.\n\nFor example, suppose that, due to overfishing, a fish population decays exponentially. The population $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = 5\\,000 \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began. What can we deduce about the population of fish from this model?\n\nThe first thing to notice is that the function above is an exponential decay function of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t.\n$$\n\nSo, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ represents the initial value. So this tells us that there were $5\\,000$ fish when the overfishing began.\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor. Since $0 < b < 1,$ this tells us that we're dealing with exponential decay, *not* exponential growth (for exponential growth we need $b > 1$).\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began." :tutorial/id #uuid "adec391e-8573-55bf-b53b-d5d15177f633" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1970 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b36f7829-7634-567d-9517-b3c22769bd8e" :step/math-academy-id 7129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115383 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114948 :question/id #uuid "df739a77-559a-53ed-ad04-be16403b9f46" :question/math-academy-id "e-2076"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeafe81f-2d40-5413-8d19-881972161400" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557278}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Output of an Exponential Decay Function"} :step/id #uuid "a9eb3a9e-cbd0-5d23-976c-d6e30621bd62" :step/math-academy-id 7131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114949 :question/id #uuid "d550f36c-a6f0-5b37-a998-4d44fa5f1d3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2078"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0f5949-6a1a-5187-a246-8f9bac2e8d58" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508951}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting a Decay Rate"} :step/id #uuid "370e94ab-be87-5775-983a-458131b0b569" :step/math-academy-id 7133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114950 :question/id #uuid "795b2573-6738-5fbd-a0f4-9d94eff7aee7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2079"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab694f0f-947f-5bba-ad7a-988ea28a97fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049657}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508967}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Exponential Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "07158a3f-d957-5396-9819-37e29b775de6" :step/math-academy-id 7137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116357}}] :activity/title "Interpreting Exponential Decay" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115438}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563814} :activity/id #uuid "641ed7b4-95b7-596b-9c95-f9f1f7a7c865" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049732 :topic/id #uuid "0f13295d-4ece-5423-b035-ea84905ef389" :topic/math-academy-id 1629} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89c513db-b3c1-5e59-ac22-9135ece7cd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114146} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c1aaf4-12ab-546f-9195-b9891d12fb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114147} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ec7b89-74ed-5ef1-b81b-13c08e162ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114148} {:answer/id #uuid "ed919af0-ab3a-59de-bb77-9c9a1a9f88d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114149} {:answer/id #uuid "2adb52f2-975c-579c-b31f-59703a1e5a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114149} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1f24b2-6672-5293-99ef-07e0e6f5b77d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "14bb2d65-c2fa-5181-9d50-ff6fa898ca5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-45602" :question/problem "The mass, in metric tons, of a meteor is modeled by the function $m(t)=20\\cdot (0.4)^{t},$ where $t$ represents the time, in minutes, since the meteor entered the Earth's atmosphere. In metric tons, how much mass did the meteor lose between the second and third minutes after entering the Earth's atmosphere?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to find the total mass that the meteor lost between $t=2$ and $t=3$ minutes. To calculate this, we find the meteor's mass after $2$ minutes, and we subtract its mass after $3$ minutes. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3).\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=2$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(2) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.16 \\\\  & =3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=3$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(3) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{3} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.064 \\\\  & =1.28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass lost between the second and third minute is\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3)=3.2-1.28=1.92.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the meteor's mass decreased by $1.92$ metric tons between the second and third minutes."} :step/id #uuid "338b5046-cbfa-5fce-92b6-aa66fdd0ef08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "120cc5f5-b994-5fe7-879c-93dded23b4f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556465} {:answer/id #uuid "62f35678-61c9-5979-beb7-a4d92e98be00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46\\,875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556656} {:answer/id #uuid "8af9a23c-445a-5275-9661-7299cac973d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\,875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556762} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee33d95-d119-584c-b8ee-7dac64f9c29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556817} {:answer/id #uuid "fb544b2f-30e9-5368-a619-27b767c38ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,675" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556656} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cb964d-8a8c-59d6-bbd2-2c379e559c33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c286c29-c804-5f9e-952f-d64fbcc60ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-45713" :question/problem "The population $C(t)$ of condors in the Andes is given by $C(t)=1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time, in decades, since the last census. How many condors vanished between the second and third decades since the last census?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the difference in the population of condors between $t=2$ and $t=3$ decades. To calculate this, we find the population after $2$ decades, and we subtract the population after $3$ decades. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=2$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.062\\,5 \\\\  & =62\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=3$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =15\\,625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the number of condors lost between the second and third decades is\n\n$$\nC(3)-C(2)=62\\,500-15\\,625=46\\,875.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $46\\,875$ condors vanished between the second and third decades."} :step/id #uuid "e1eed6a1-910c-5744-b476-f16f45177e2a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9131f976-07ea-52cc-9cba-99a846e52f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The car increases its value by $85%$ each year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114134} {:answer/id #uuid "fbde3adb-07da-5f3e-9471-c7c404c031e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The car increases its value by $15%$ each year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114135} {:answer/id #uuid "15751958-c03e-595e-bf69-e4c3ce7d40fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114136} {:answer/id #uuid "16c7b050-fa51-5bed-8e60-38696acb397a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The value of the car depreciates by $85%$ each year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114137} {:answer/id #uuid "f92c8393-663a-5dac-89bc-0621191f3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The initial price of the car is $\\$25\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114136} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32a33fa-fb7f-52f5-a0c8-9f50caf27e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3b529d12-30a0-50ea-a8c0-3efc03b69cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-45455" :question/problem "The market value of a sports car, in dollars, can be modeled according to the rule $30\\,000\\cdot (0.85)^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the car was manufactured. What does the base of the exponential expression $(0.85)$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=30\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n\n- $1-r=0.85,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observations, in years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.85\\,⟹\\,r=0.15.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.15$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $15%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.85$ means that the value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year."} :step/id #uuid "f7af5296-e946-52d2-bb41-cfb46710f3b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563775}}] :activity/title "Interpreting Exponential Decay" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562930}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180354} :activity/id #uuid "48809555-7500-5dc1-824d-52a5a22edeba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580 :topic/id #uuid "557f8b58-be49-555e-8162-3d1448eaff0c" :topic/math-academy-id 3728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180785 :tutorial/content "The range of a function can be thought of as the set of $y$-values given by the function's graph. For that reason, it's often helpful to plot the function when determining its range.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function \n\n$$\nf(x) =3-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any real number. What is the range of $f(x)$?\n\nSince this is a linear function, we only need two points to plot it. For example:\n\n- When $x=0,$ we have $y=3.$ This gives the point $(0,3).$\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have $y=1.$ This gives the point $(1,1).$\n\nPlotting these two points and connecting them, we obtain the following diagram.\n\n![](images/51/5124e487118191ae40248d4488450d065fe71712f709ac62321d749ac17b0127.png)\n\nNotice that the graph $y=f(x)$ will cover all possible $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty <          y <          \\infty.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $(-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a linear function with a restricted domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a33428f-024c-5783-b5d1-b4b0f342d4a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8800 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b396314a-88fd-58e7-a7b9-40fb4292040b" :step/math-academy-id 192940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179477 :question/id #uuid "dc5cefa9-aa18-52d3-bbdc-a0c1050f6f3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14168"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050790}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 175921