uestion/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f5d6b2e7-1b54-5bc0-b6ea-58d113518a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-236465" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fae9ac16ebabea7054903ab75f2305f98c757f8ee7fc4924600774e627ad76b4.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n$\\,$  The range is $f(x)\\in $\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (-3,0]$ is $[-2,0)$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (0,3)$ is $(1,3]$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-2,0)\\cup (1,3].$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the values $y=0$ and $y=1$ are NOT valid outputs of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use parentheses $(…)$ instead of brackets $[…]$ at these endpoints."} :step/id #uuid "b4b9a850-1b05-55cc-93b4-fc159fb75d80"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d05cb0d-8d25-5449-ac1c-8b614908715e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2<f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489543} {:answer/id #uuid "1b96b9f5-5dee-542b-9536-5184c253d217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489577} {:answer/id #uuid "536d01f7-fbaf-515c-9cab-bbacb8844c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<f(x)\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489711} {:answer/id #uuid "61686cce-e8eb-5e02-8245-808eec7d2bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\le f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489744} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d3bfaa-3cdb-5dfc-8e12-4bf0044664fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490050} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c513d9a-67e4-587c-bfd8-0a60d03dc406" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30dbc182-568e-5645-9ba2-4447169ef008" :question/math-academy-id "q-129372" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb292b77908dbb4590b6bbc49c452215edd8e6668e003e0984074a7ca22bf4ff.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs when $x=2.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $\\infty ,$ including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as  $0\\le f(x)<\\infty .$"} :step/id #uuid "bff636d5-d253-5f8b-b379-4b8793e7f844" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490348}}] :activity/title "The Range of a Function: Advanced Cases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489508}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069552} :activity/id #uuid "a71fc11d-26f8-54c9-8cba-9f88ebf49ad8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050734 :topic/id #uuid "6256411f-5caf-56bd-8434-2969fbc3f5f1" :topic/math-academy-id 388} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070018 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to solve the compound inequality \n\n$$\n4 \\le x + 2 \\le 8.\n$$\n\nTo solve the compound inequality, we apply the usual operations to the middle of the inequality to isolate $x.$ Keep in mind that whatever we do to the middle, we also need to do to *both* the left-hand *and* right-hand sides.\n\nHere, we can isolate $x$ by subtracting $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t4 & \\le x + 2 \\le 8 \\[5pt]\n\t4 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} &\\le x + 2 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\le 8 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\[5pt]\n\t2 &\\le x \\le 6\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $2 \\le x \\le 6.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d8fae47-5e67-5793-a585-d3781d5c4555" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8677 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "83b17106-05a3-58df-89b9-4510e4dbee9f" :step/math-academy-id 108821 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068472 :question/id #uuid "0901e241-519b-50ee-ab5f-50b2f631e948" :question/math-academy-id "e-13932"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7229f1d0-4ba7-50ff-b5be-5697c886a175" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507422}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Compound Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "7ff1eeef-0d3b-5cde-9aca-5461fbfa7dee" :step/math-academy-id 108822 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068686 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068473 :question/id #uuid "8208b5ba-8c54-5c05-a9f4-04aa30f8892f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13933"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5930113e-6971-59c9-810a-97fd25bc4f89" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507425}] :knowledge-point/title "Compound Inequalities That Require Flipping the Inequality Sign"} :step/id #uuid "60d28d8b-03d2-535c-826f-3510ebac457f" :step/math-academy-id 108825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068474 :question/id #uuid "7f671658-055a-599d-8f1f-416570e604b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-13935"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3ff37f0-6e45-5da4-ac87-1b93c8df3e80" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507522}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Solutions of Compound Inequalities on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "5885620e-83f2-528b-862e-d400efe5fd7c" :step/math-academy-id 108828}] :activity/title "Solving Compound Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068935}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542268} :activity/id #uuid "3d86730f-81e7-5064-8ba6-3666740e5cdc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050734 :topic/id #uuid "6256411f-5caf-56bd-8434-2969fbc3f5f1" :topic/math-academy-id 388} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93615638-cbb7-575b-b00d-202bb80ca84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c2d44907a96db2c56e169dd101eaa12fb30708071ff9f89bace604a5debc0a11.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067283} {:answer/id #uuid "a9367703-0297-550c-a9e0-af792bc9e4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c4/c4f4ab05b534287dc75765b83dc23cbdfe42044aeddea220510668fbc6b8b51c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067284} {:answer/id #uuid "e6acad9f-7677-51ae-a16e-82c2a29fec7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ec/ec2388438d6e5e5d0b48aba6933a4f21d2ed6bc9680d205a01529fd51d1be3d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067285} {:answer/id #uuid "fa34fae2-0e28-5995-859c-524ffeeb539b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1d/1d5c3ba6ce6cf5dc1cd628b7ecc9e2c1226df58ab52b140b8fb800057a867b90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067286} {:answer/id #uuid "fc684b81-529b-5570-87fd-3dbad8f8cd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c5/c5e7b54c1556558d66fd6f62d40c4b1c92351546f257b86c8b024019375fce78.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186067284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4bf358a-d06e-5e6d-a366-ff3bf78482b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc79035c-5b95-5816-b7f8-1b0d00701572" :question/math-academy-id "q-44758" :question/problem "Which of the following number lines gives the solution to $-5\\le -2x+3\\le 5?$" :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual. \n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & \\le -2x+3\\le 5 \\\\ -5-3 & \\le -2x+3-3\\le 5-3 \\\\ -8 & \\le -2x\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-2.$ Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & \\le -2x\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{-8}{-2} & \\ge \\frac{-2x}{-2}\\ge \\frac{2}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{8}{2} & \\ge x\\ge -\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ 4 & \\ge x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $4\\ge x\\ge -1$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: \n\n$$\n-1\\le x\\le 4\n$$\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n![](images/c4/c4f4ab05b534287dc75765b83dc23cbdfe42044aeddea220510668fbc6b8b51c.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1253c382-2d7e-5a16-9680-26c02ce78e50"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b88ace-9390-57a4-b822-7e9f4d518606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}<x<\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541494} {:answer/id #uuid "2624632d-58c6-55cc-a5ce-f1a07f1948b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13<x<17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541500} {:answer/id #uuid "60c48b0d-8516-5c9c-b0c0-ca3b48e4e6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{3}<x<-\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541620} {:answer/id #uuid "709295a7-9d6f-5c26-b22c-811e6b3cb1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{3}<x<\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541651} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a50cfa-4654-50d6-90c0-f285448b821d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13<x<17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541494} :answer-field/id #uuid "c27cbefd-6954-5311-a2c3-8fc6b2f31576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "793dd364-450f-5a57-9269-620a4ed6531f" :question/math-academy-id "q-3063" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-9<-3x+8<-5.$" :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual. \n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & <-3x+8<-5 \\\\ -9-8 & <-3x+8-8<-5-8 \\\\ -17 & <-3x<-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-3.$ Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-17 & <-3x<-13 \\\\ \\frac{-17}{-3} & >\\frac{-3x}{-3}>\\frac{-13}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{3} & >x>\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe solution can be written as $\\frac{17}{3}>x>\\frac{13}{3}$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: \n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{3}<x<\\frac{17}{3}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "85064973-2757-5296-84d2-6df053d92684" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9110478c-a991-53de-9d31-a7f629a5ad37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1a309b8253794909323ba60425c22767a428033b1a9ae7ce406cbff737dc285e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067277} {:answer/id #uuid "a09f76aa-8712-561f-8788-30d2c2a1e487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b5/b55856fc8211d51c63e00e050ee0b17678208a6c7fd2b106fc2e9a37f43c1fec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067278} {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2028-f8bd-59fe-afdf-6077c2a88594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e8/e825e9ccefdbbc09785b5fc1c877d3f8dc039740f4e2d5ad84243dafead1ce19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067279} {:answer/id #uuid "060ef09e-7f48-5ab1-9c2e-4bea9ff49fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/76/76b6a19b3104f122989479a681709af3ca0bf8c05c59f76dc2c7707643980398.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067280} {:answer/id #uuid "44440598-321a-51a4-9930-c9e22cfd29d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/30/3092ffa9f2feead2598880f35fd7960266b755b10534728a56a5dfd0d49c1054.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186067280} :answer-field/id #uuid "950294dc-00bb-558f-ba1d-e7ce2be43e02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a50fbeb7-374f-57aa-97cb-71f55ec85260" :question/math-academy-id "q-44745" :question/problem "Which of the following number lines gives the solution to $-3\\le 2x-1\\le 5?$" :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$  by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\le 2x-1\\le 5 \\\\ -3+1 & \\le 2x-1+1\\le 5+1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2x\\le 6 \\\\ \\frac{-2}{2} & \\le \\frac{2x}{2}\\le \\frac{6}{2} \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows. \n\n![](images/76/76b6a19b3104f122989479a681709af3ca0bf8c05c59f76dc2c7707643980398.png)"} :step/id #uuid "c9ea2740-6125-59b0-b9c0-691a5f0f366c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542235}}] :activity/title "Solving Compound Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541419}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065109} :activity/id #uuid "7e0946df-45c0-5786-9c65-795f6164a618" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818 :topic/id #uuid "68e64e22-0e3c-5eac-8837-c36b21dd36c0" :topic/math-academy-id 371} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065440 :tutorial/content "When we want to multiply two binomials, we take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x + 2),$ we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x + 2),$ and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x+2)$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}{x}}(x + 2) + {\\color{red}{3}}(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 2x + 3x + 6 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 5x + 6\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0cea50d1-e4ce-511f-821f-0e5e04b6eca1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8877 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "034d8161-5ef0-5329-ac71-ca2e17506a54" :step/math-academy-id 171384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063988 :question/id #uuid "ab6787e7-5b47-51a6-8967-4ede296cc44f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14313"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ff8de23-e4f2-51e1-9390-78ff7edd2176" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049538}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481382}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Binomials"} :step/id #uuid "befc51ec-1128-57f9-9017-8b1297e2a304" :step/math-academy-id 171385 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064242 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063989 :question/id #uuid "818a9bf5-5ed5-5809-a295-3d332ce98b47" :question/math-academy-id "e-14316"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36d4e3a1-9f2e-5434-bfc9-10c29a426e07" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495155}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Binomials with Leading Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "cd84d1a6-b898-5206-86c9-b0b4524beab8" :step/math-academy-id 171388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063990 :question/id #uuid "4e99bb73-42a1-5b11-8463-020f297c5afc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27aef839-e6a9-5c8e-bcd7-a2e9438968b0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048422}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542135}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Binomials with Higher Powers and Multiple Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f2afcd5e-f99f-58d4-92e1-abd2be293082" :step/math-academy-id 171391}] :activity/title "Multiplying Binomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064457}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495266} :activity/id #uuid "8721e25f-828d-529f-8d37-00132303e9c8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818 :topic/id #uuid "68e64e22-0e3c-5eac-8837-c36b21dd36c0" :topic/math-academy-id 371} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495211 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e004be-f08e-52db-afcf-7bfb57aca531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{w}^{2}+19w-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494390} {:answer/id #uuid "33f29d9f-b7f9-538d-84ef-87f102db7a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{w}^{2}-19w-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494421} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1a226c-3b58-5de1-bdd1-09862e44a749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{w}^{2}-31w-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494467} {:answer/id #uuid "725a4025-c76c-5f78-bc98-4504976b2a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{w}^{2}+19w-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494562} {:answer/id #uuid "c9194380-fd78-526b-86b8-a0353fc773b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{w}^{2}-31w-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494421} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdeb2f7f-39f3-55df-bda0-3ce7a30bfa82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0944dac9-480d-52ed-acdf-aeb980d5e726" :question/math-academy-id "q-52152" :question/problem "$(5w+2)(3w-5)=$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5w+2)(3w-5) & = \\\\ 5w(3w-5)+2(3w-5) & = \\\\ 15{w}^{2}-25w+6w-10 & = \\\\ 15{w}^{2}-19w-10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6dab3f05-590c-5fd9-986b-e9d3f2e3114c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2a21ab-44c4-5643-851b-b4a76932cc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16y^{2}+42y+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494344} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00f9b37-087b-5f93-99a9-aa065185418d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e2cad58f-c472-581c-a16b-711a6072cc50" :question/math-academy-id "q-216712" :question/problem "Expand the following product of binomials. Write your answer in **standard form.**\n\n$(8y+1)(2y+5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8y+1)(2y+5) & = \\\\ 8y(2y+5)+1(2y+5) & = \\\\ 16{y}^{2}+40y+2y+5 & = \\\\ 16{y}^{2}+42y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d0ea6c41-f392-560c-af01-cf23e5e44c12"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b16ee72-72ca-517a-b1b1-2198b148afbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}+rs-2s{r}^{2}-2{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494371} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7ad74c-af73-5446-9052-df37d91b1f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}+r{s}^{2}-2s{r}^{2}-{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494544} {:answer/id #uuid "759904a1-8636-537e-9c40-516d7deaa4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}+r{s}^{2}-2s{r}^{2}+2{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494568} {:answer/id #uuid "907ffe26-5485-53ae-a1fa-c8f1197ef46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}+r{s}^{2}-2s{r}^{2}-2{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494646} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0f2020-3fca-5d25-b5e4-b0d419345bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}+rs-2s{r}^{2}+{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494646} :answer-field/id #uuid "6164af12-a061-5247-be0b-02a0af340dc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f37487a-b4b8-5992-9edf-d3983de67b65" :question/math-academy-id "q-31319" :question/problem "$(r-2s)({r}^{2}+{s}^{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-2s)({r}^{2}+{s}^{2}) & = \\\\ r({r}^{2}+{s}^{2})+(-2s)({r}^{2}+{s}^{2}) & = \\\\ {r}^{3}+r{s}^{2}-2s{r}^{2}-2{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms."} :step/id #uuid "eb20a321-9a03-525a-8fd7-cf559bae04c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495211}}] :activity/title "Multiplying Binomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494307}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060674} :activity/id #uuid "780b6d06-4e3c-5fb9-a687-1ad2687e0771" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050953 :topic/id #uuid "7525f3f4-fbe5-5024-8fab-55ad3066edd8" :topic/math-academy-id 266} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059519 :question/id #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503715}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "a0e30d07-9c41-5c2c-839c-9e49ca64764c" :step/math-academy-id 15458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059520 :question/id #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540075}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "233132be-0fc4-52d1-9152-818b8c45b168" :step/math-academy-id 15459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059521 :question/id #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4235"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503729}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "13e292aa-c881-5526-9904-6d174bbb856b" :step/math-academy-id 15460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :question/id #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4236"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "634f203a-900b-5f9f-977b-e1bb2ab2c5ab" :step/math-academy-id 15461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060937 :tutorial/content "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x \\\\ 10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "33bd96be-8ef8-525c-be19-8590b4893b52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3123 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b043ea3a-fd3a-5de4-b7ac-21a29627ca53" :step/math-academy-id 17016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060580}}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059941}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540116} :activity/id #uuid "1c136286-07e7-54b0-87c2-d0c00e24e994" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050953 :topic/id #uuid "7525f3f4-fbe5-5024-8fab-55ad3066edd8" :topic/math-academy-id 266} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe93af-c815-5f2c-ac75-9a584d561ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058879} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d50060-ed5a-55ba-98a5-e136d622f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7edad-f24a-5a39-b3a9-bdf0e222d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058881} {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} {:answer/id #uuid "4335e387-f8cb-50d1-a3a5-f1a739c9876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} :answer-field/id #uuid "084ec80c-c0ed-58a4-b621-614f03d00659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09320d7a-d867-531b-9740-66504912149d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67332" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04bd31c0-ddc2-5b9c-ad07-d73251e35927" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d56b3b6-d2ee-5a6a-ac79-f166c602aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539536} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc6a18-26ef-5ee7-a216-046a93109cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539634} {:answer/id #uuid "684d8919-d630-53ec-be88-da83985b9cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539650} {:answer/id #uuid "adf72623-7732-5460-b0b6-5cc1011adb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539775} {:answer/id #uuid "f71975b1-e674-5e94-a227-f47c16e02797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894} :answer-field/id #uuid "746714c2-cc0f-5a43-9606-b934d8793273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dba16e2f-f755-5c6f-b25b-801d348b9282" :question/math-academy-id "q-67435" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{4}{x-2}=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{x-1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $4.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{4x-4}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{-4}) & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{4x-4}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ x+4x & =4+2 \\\\ 5x & =6 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (5x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "128aaed4-1578-549e-bc3a-0ca44706efdb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e054d1-ef35-5151-b5a6-95077b0323c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539572} {:answer/id #uuid "5deed0da-0520-5246-8e6c-365d802468b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539637} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5af59e-eefa-5320-9d2c-7a4fb55193a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539660} {:answer/id #uuid "a816dc9c-912f-5b96-8ae4-1b55000c74ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539765} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e754c7-eafd-5c39-98a7-4f2732e90d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539660} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7cb7b4d-8e3a-52e0-b39c-57977a95c75f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9929bc33-2631-5ab2-871b-80ee305fc700" :question/math-academy-id "q-67413" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+5=3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =3 \\\\ x+5-5 & =3-5 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5a1efe66-123d-5aba-9cc1-514789a8cab9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540123}}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539465}]}
